---
title: "Publish from NATS JetStream"
description: "Configure self-hosted Outpost to consume events from an existing NATS JetStream stream."
---

Configure self-hosted Outpost to consume events from an existing NATS JetStream stream and route them to matching destinations. Messages use the same event structure as the [Publish API](/docs/outpost/publishing/events).

## Message structure

Each message body must be a JSON object with the fields used by the Publish API:

```json
{
  "tenant_id": "tenant_123",
  "topic": "user.created",
  "data": {
    "user_id": "usr_456"
  }
}
```

`tenant_id` is required. Set `topic` to match a destination subscription, or set `destination_id` to route to one destination directly. See [publishing events](/docs/outpost/publishing/events#event-structure) for all supported fields.

## Configure Outpost

Outpost requires the NATS server URL, an existing stream name, and the subject to consume. It creates or updates a durable pull consumer on that stream. Outpost does not create, update, or delete the stream.

### Environment variables

```sh
PUBLISH_NATS_SERVER_URL="nats://nats.example.com:4222"
PUBLISH_NATS_STREAM="outpost-publish"
PUBLISH_NATS_SUBJECT="outpost-publish"
PUBLISH_NATS_CONSUMER="outpost-publish-consumer"
```

`PUBLISH_NATS_CONSUMER` is optional and defaults to `outpost-publish-consumer`. Use a dedicated consumer for this Outpost subscription. Outpost replicas configured with the same stream, subject, and consumer share that durable consumer; use different consumer names when separate Outpost deployments each need to receive every event.

### YAML

```yaml
publishmq:
  nats:
    server_url: "nats://nats.example.com:4222"
    stream: "outpost-publish"
    subject: "outpost-publish"
    consumer: "outpost-publish-consumer"
```

Create the stream and ensure its subject configuration includes the subject Outpost will consume. The consumer name must contain only letters, numbers, hyphens, or underscores.

If the stream uses `WorkQueuePolicy`, no other durable consumer can have an overlapping filter for this subject. JetStream work-queue retention does not allow the same message to be assigned to multiple consumers.

## Delivery and redelivery

Outpost acknowledges a message after it has accepted the event and enqueued its matching destination deliveries. Event delivery to each destination then follows Outpost's normal retry policy.

The JetStream consumer uses explicit acknowledgements and permits unlimited broker redeliveries. A failed event is negatively acknowledged and JetStream can redeliver it immediately. `PUBLISH_MAX_REDELIVERIES` can cap processing failures in Outpost; when that limit is reached, Outpost acknowledges the message and stops retrying it.

## Availability

This publish-queue configuration is for self-hosted Outpost. It is separate from `mqs.nats`, which configures NATS JetStream for Outpost's internal delivery and logging queues.

If the publish consumer cannot subscribe or repeatedly fails to receive messages, `/healthz` reports its status. You can enable worker restarts by adding `publishmq` to `SUPERVISOR_RESTART_WORKERS`. See [Supervisor configuration](/docs/outpost/self-hosting/configuration#supervisor).
